nodes.statement:
Let
\setlength{\leftmargini}{0.4cm}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\pi_{m-1}:\mathrm{X}\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{m-1}$ be the quotient map, which is also open.
    \item $\pi_{m-1}^{\db{m}\setminus\{1^m\}}:\mathrm{Cor}^m(\mathrm{X})\rightarrow\mathrm{Cor}^m(\mathrm{X}_{m-1})$ be the coordinate-wise application of $\pi_{m-1}$ (which is continuous).
    \item $\mathcal{K}_m:\mathrm{Cor}^m(\mathrm{X}_{m-1})\rightarrow \mathrm{X}_{m-1}$ the completion function (which is continuous by hypothesis).

For $n>k$, note that we can write the map $\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}}$ as the composition of the following maps:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes,row sep=3em,column sep=4em,minimum width=2em]
  {\mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X}) & \hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}) & \hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}_{k-1}) & \mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X}_{k-1}) \\};
  \path[-stealth]
    (m-1-1) edge node [above] {$p_{\db{n}_{\le k}}$} (m-1-2)
    (m-1-2) edge node [above] {$\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}_{\le k}}$} (m-1-3)
    (m-1-3) edge node [above] {$\mathcal{K}'$} (m-1-4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Here $p_{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is defined as in Lemma \ref{lem:simplicial-proy}, $\db{n}_{\le k}:=\{v\in\db{n}:\sum_{j=1}^n v\sbr{j}\le k\}$, $\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is the coordinate-wise application of $\pi_{k-1}$ and $\mathcal{K}'$ is the composition of several applications of the completion function $\mathcal{K}:\mathrm{Cor}^k(\mathrm{X}_{k-1})\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{k-1}$ in order to complete an element of $\hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}_{k-1})$. By Lemma \ref{lem:simplicial-proy} we have that $p_{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is open. By an argument very similar to the one in the previous paragraph we have that $\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is open as well, and $\mathcal{K}'$ is open as its inverse is continuous (because it is just a projection to some coordinates).

For $n>k$, note that we can write the map $\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}}$ as the composition of the following maps:
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes,row sep=3em,column sep=4em,minimum width=2em]
  {\mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X}) & \hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}) & \hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}_{k-1}) & \mathrm{C}^n(\mathrm{X}_{k-1}) \\};
  \path[-stealth]
    (m-1-1) edge node [above] {$p_{\db{n}_{\le k}}$} (m-1-2)
    (m-1-2) edge node [above] {$\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}_{\le k}}$} (m-1-3)
    (m-1-3) edge node [above] {$\mathcal{K}'$} (m-1-4);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
Here $p_{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is defined as in Lemma \ref{lem:simplicial-proy}, $\db{n}_{\le k}:=\{v\in\db{n}:\sum_{j=1}^n v\sbr{j}\le k\}$, $\pi_{k-1}^{\db{n}_{\le k}}$ is the coordinate-wise application of $\pi_{k-1}$ and $\mathcal{K}'$ is the composition of several applications of the completion function $\mathcal{K}:\mathrm{Cor}^k(\mathrm{X}_{k-1})\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{k-1}$ in order to complete an element of $\hom({\db{n}_{\le k}},\mathrm{X}_{k-1})$.

For $i< k$, we already have a purely algebraic nilspace isomorphism $(\mathrm{X} \times_{\mathrm{Y}} \mathrm{X}')_i \rightarrow \mathrm{X}_i \times_{\mathrm{Y}_i} \mathrm{X}_i'$. If we show that this is also a homeomorphism then we will be done, as we can then repeat the preceding argument to obtain the continuity of the completion function. To obtain the homeomorphism property it suffices to show that the quotient topology on $(\mathrm{X} \times_{\mathrm{Y}} \mathrm{X}')_i$ is equal to the subspace topology on $\mathrm{X}_i \times_{\mathrm{Y}_i} \mathrm{X}_i'$ induced by the product topology on $\mathrm{X}_i\times\mathrm{X}_i$. Note that it suffices to show this for $i=k-1$, as the rest of the cases follow by induction on $k$. Hence, let us consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}[c]
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes,row sep=2em,column sep=4em,minimum width=2em]
  {\mathrm{X} \times_{\mathrm{Y}} \mathrm{X}' & \\
     (\mathrm{X} \times_{\mathrm{Y}} \mathrm{X}')_{k-1} & \mathrm{X}_{k-1} \times_{\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}} \mathrm{X}_{k-1}', \\};
  \path[-stealth]
    (m-1-1) edge node [above] {$T$} (m-2-2)
    (m-1-1) edge node [right] {$\pi$} (m-2-1)
    (m-2-1) edge node [above] {$\iota$} (m-2-2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\vspace{-0.3cm}

\noindent where $T(x,x'):=(\pi_{k-1}(x),\pi_{k-1}(x'))$, $\pi$ is the quotient map for the relation $\sim_k$ (see \cite[Definition 3.2.3]{Cand:Notes1}) and $\iota(\pi(x,x'))=(\pi_{k-1}(x),\pi_{k-1}(x'))$. Recall that by \cite[Proposition A.21]{CGSS-p-hom}, $\iota$ is a well-defined bijective morphism. We want to prove that it is a homeomorphism.

