Recall that by \cite[Proposition A.19]{CGSS-p-hom} the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}[c]
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes,row sep=3em,column sep=4em,minimum width=2em]
  {\mathrm{Y} & \mathrm{X}  \\
     \mathrm{Y}_{k-1} & \mathrm{X}_{k-1}, \\};
  \path[-stealth]
    (m-1-1) edge node [above] {$q$} (m-1-2)
    (m-1-1) edge node [right] {$\pi$} (m-2-1)
    (m-1-2) edge node [right] {$\pi$} (m-2-2)
    (m-2-1) edge node [above] {$q_{k-1}$} (m-2-2);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\vspace{-0.3cm}

\noindent where $q_{k-1}$ defines a degree-$t$ extension of $\mathrm{X}_{k-1}$. (We just write $\pi$ instead of $\pi_{k-1}$ to avoid overloading the notation; the space where $\pi$ is defined will be clear from the context.)

Finally, let us prove that $(iii)$ holds. Let $(\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \mathrm{c}_n',\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \mathrm{c}_n)\in \Delta_{q_{k-1}:\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{k-1}}$ be a sequence converging to some limit $(\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}',\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c})\in \Delta_{q_{k-1}:\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{k-1}}$. We want to prove that the completions of the corners $\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}_n'$ converge as well. By Lemma \ref{lem:conv-quot} there exists $d_n\in\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})^{\db{t+1}\setminus\{1^{t+1}\}}=\mathrm{Cor}^{t+1}(\mathcal{D}_k(\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})))$ such that $\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n\rightarrow \mathrm{c}'$ in $\mathrm{Cor}^{t+1}(\mathrm{Y})$. Then, clearly $q\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} (\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)$ is a lift of $\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}_n|_{\db{t+1}\setminus\{1^{t+1}\}}$. Applying again Lemma \ref{lem:conv-quot}, this time to $\mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})$, we get a sequence of elements $z_n\in \mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{X})$ such that $\mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})+z_n\rightarrow \mathrm{c}(1^{t+1})$. Now for each $n$ let $\mathrm{c}^*_n: 
v\mapsto \begin{cases}
 q\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v) & \text{ if }v\not=1^{t+1}\\
 \mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})+z_n & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}$. By \cite[Remark 3.2.12]{Cand:Notes1} (using that $t<k$) we have $\mathrm{c}^*_n\in \mathrm{C}^{t+1}(\mathrm{X})$ for every $n$. Furthermore, these cubes converge to some limit $\mathrm{c}^*\in \mathrm{C}^{t+1}(\mathrm{X})$ such that $\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}^*=\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}$. Indeed, for $v\not=1^{t+1}$ we have $\mathrm{c}^*_n(v)=q(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v)$ which converges because $(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v)$ converges and $q$ is continuous, and $\mathrm{c}^*_n(1^{t+1})=\mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})+z_n$, which converges by assumption (the resulting $\mathrm{c}^*$ is a cube again by \cite[Remark 3.2.12]{Cand:Notes1}). Thus, we can apply the fiber-completion continuity to the sequence $(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n,\mathrm{c}_n^*)$. The limit of the completions is the completion of their limit, i.e.\ $(\mathrm{c}',\mathrm{c}^*)\in \Delta_{q:\mathrm{Y}\rightarrow\mathrm{X}}$. Letting  $\mathcal{K}_{(q_{k-1})}$ be the completion function in $\Delta_{q_{k-1}:\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}\rightarrow\mathrm{X}_{k-1}}$, note that  $\mathcal{K}_{(q_{k-1})}(\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}_n',\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}_n) = \pi(\mathcal{K}_{(q)}(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n,\mathrm{c}_n^*))$, and the latter, by continuity of $\pi$ and $\mathcal{K}_{(q)}$, converges to $\pi(\mathcal{K}_{(q)}(\mathrm{c}',\mathrm{c}^*))$. Arguing as in the previous sentence, we have $\pi(\mathcal{K}_{(q)}(\mathrm{c}',\mathrm{c}^*)) = \mathcal{K}_{(q_{k-1})}(\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}',\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}^*) = \mathcal{K}_{(q_{k-1})}(\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}',\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c})$, and $(iii)$ follows.

Now for each $n$ let $\mathrm{c}^*_n: 
v\mapsto \begin{cases}
 q\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v) & \text{ if }v\not=1^{t+1}\\
 \mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})+z_n & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}$. By \cite[Remark 3.2.12]{Cand:Notes1} (using that $t<k$) we have $\mathrm{c}^*_n\in \mathrm{C}^{t+1}(\mathrm{X})$ for every $n$. Furthermore, these cubes converge to some limit $\mathrm{c}^*\in \mathrm{C}^{t+1}(\mathrm{X})$ such that $\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}^*=\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\mathrm{c}$. Indeed, for $v\not=1^{t+1}$ we have $\mathrm{c}^*_n(v)=q(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v)$ which converges because $(\mathrm{c}_n'+d_n)(v)$ converges and $q$ is continuous, and $\mathrm{c}^*_n(1^{t+1})=\mathrm{c}_n(1^{t+1})+z_n$, which converges by assumption (the resulting $\mathrm{c}^*$ is a cube again by \cite[Remark 3.2.12]{Cand:Notes1}).

First we address the discrete part, in which $A$ is just a finite abelian group. In this case, letting $r$ be the rank of $A$, the conclusion is the following:
\begin{equation} \textrm{there exists a morphism $\psi:F\rightarrow \mathcal{D}_k(\mathbb{Z}^r)$ such that $\phi=\pi\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\psi$.}   
\end{equation}
Indeed, it suffices to take the expansion \eqref{eq:Taylor-discrete} for $\phi$, and lift it to a Taylor expansion of a morphism $\psi:F\rightarrow \mathcal{D}_k(B=\mathbb{Z}^r)$ by replacing each coefficient $g_m\in A$ by some $g_m'\in B$ such that $\pi(g_m')=g_m$.

Next we treat the continuous part of the problem, where the image group $A$ is a torus $\mathbb{T}^s$. Letting $\pi:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{T}$ be the canonical quotient homomorphism, the main claim here is that
\begin{equation} \textrm{there exists a morphism $\psi:F\rightarrow \mathcal{D}_k(\mathbb{R}^s)$ such that $\phi=\pi^s\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}\psi$.}
\end{equation}
This follows readily by applying Lemma \ref{lem:Taylor-cont} to each of the $s$ coordinate maps of $\phi$ and taking the product of the resulting maps.

Given a $k$-step nilspace $\mathrm{X}$, recall from \cite[Definition 3.3.1 (ii)]{Cand:Notes1} that for every $j\in [k]$ we have the following group homomorphism (e.g.\ by \cite[Lemma 1.5]{CGSS} with $\psi=\pi_{k-1}$): 
\begin{equation} \eta_j:\Theta(\mathrm{X})\rightarrow \Theta(\mathrm{X}_j),\quad \alpha\mapsto \alpha_j,\textrm{ where } \alpha_j\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \pi_j = \pi_j\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} \alpha,
\end{equation}

We argue by induction on $k$. The case $k=1$ is given by Lemma ef{lem:abcasesplit}. For $k>1$, we assume that the result holds for step at most $k-1$. The crux of the proof consists in specifying a continuous cross-section $\mathrm{s}:Fightarrow \mathrm{Y}$. Fixing some $y\in q^{-1}(0)$, we will obtain from each element $f\in F$ a translation on $\mathrm{Y}$ in a consistent way, defining then $\mathrm{s}(f)$ as the image of $y$ under this translation. Before detailing the above construction of $\mathrm{s}$, we make the following observation that uses the inductive hypothesis: for any $t\le k-1$, and any free nilspace $F'$ (of arbitrary finite step), any degree-$t$ extension $\psi:\mathrm{Y}'ightarrow F'$ splits. This observation is essentially \cite[Lemma 4.4]{CGSS-p-hom}; for completeness we detail the proof here. Consider the following commutative diagram:  egin{equation}  egin{aligned}[c]  egin{tikzpicture} \matrix (m) [matrix of math nodes,row sep=2em,column sep=4em,minimum width=2em] {\mathrm{Y}' & F' \ \mathrm{Y}'_{t} & F'_{t}. \}; \path[-stealth] (m-1-1) edge node [above] {$\psi$} (m-1-2) (m-1-1) edge node [right] {$\pi_t$} (m-2-1) (m-1-2) edge node [right] {$\pi_t$} (m-2-2) (m-2-1) edge node [above] {$\psi_t$} (m-2-2); \end{tikzpicture} \end{aligned} \end{equation} \vspace{-0.3cm} 
oindent By \cite[Proposition A.19]{CGSS-p-hom} we already know that $\mathrm{Y}'$ is isomorphic (algebraically) to the nilspace $\mathrm{Y}'_t	imes_{F'_t} F'$, the isomorphism being the map $y\mapsto (\pi_t(y),\psi(y))$. As this map is a continuous fibration between Lie-fibered nilspaces, it is an open map by Theorem ef{thm:open-mapping-thm}. Hence it is a homeomorphism. By the induction hypothesis, the extension $\psi_t:\mathrm{Y}'_tightarrow F'_t$ splits, so there exists a continuous cross-section $s_t:F'_tightarrow \mathrm{Y}'_t$ such that $\psi_t\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} s_t=\mathrm{id}$. Then, the map $F'ightarrow \mathrm{Y}'_t	imes_{F'_t} F'$, $x\mapsto(s_t(\pi_t(x)),x)$ is a continuous cross-section from $F'$ to $\mathrm{Y}'$. Now let $q:\mathrm{Y}ightarrow F$ be the continuous fibration given in the theorem. Note that $q_{k-1}$ is then an isomorphism $\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}ightarrow F_{k-1}$ (by similar arguments as in the proof of Proposition ef{prop:ext-lie-is-lie-1}). This implies that for every translation $\alpha\in\Theta(\mathrm{Y})$ the map $q$ is \emph{$\alpha$-consistent}, meaning that $q(y)=q(y')\Rightarrow q(\alpha y)=q(\alpha y')$. Indeed, if $q(y)=q(y')$, then from $q_{k-1}$ being an isomorphism it follows that $y,y'$ are in the same fiber of $\pi_{k-1,\mathrm{Y}}$, so $y'=y+z$ for some $z\in\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})$, and then $q(y)=q(y')$ also implies that $z$ is in the kernel of the $k$-th structure homomorphism $\phi_{k,q}$ of $q$ (see \cite[Definition 3.3.1]{Cand:Notes1}). Then $\alpha (y')=\alpha(y)+ z$, so $q(\alpha y')=q(\alpha(y))+ \phi_{k,q}(z)=q(\alpha(y))$ as claimed. We can then define (as in \cite[Lemma 1.5]{CGSS}) a homomorphism $\widehat{q}:\Theta(\mathrm{Y}) ightarrow \Theta(F)$, which is a \emph{filtered} group homomorphism, i.e.\ such that for each $i\in [k]$, $\widehat{q}$ sends $\alpha\in \Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ to the translation $\widehat{q}(\alpha): x\in F\mapsto q(\alpha(y))$ in $\Theta_i(F)$ (for any $y\in \mathrm{Y}$ with $q(y)=x$). To obtain the desired cross-section $\mathrm{s}$ below, we shall use the following properties of $\widehat{q}$. First note that $\widehat{q}$ is continuous (relative to the compact-open topology on $\Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ and $\Theta_i(F)$; recall \S ef{sec:trans-group}). Indeed, suppose for a contradiction that there was a sequence $(\alpha_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ converging to $\alpha$ in $\Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ such that $\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)
otightarrow \widehat{q}(\alpha)$. Then there would be a compact set $K\subset F$ and some $\epsilon>0$ such that $\sup_{x\in K}d_F(\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)(x),\widehat{q}(\alpha)(x))>\epsilon$ for all $n$. Thus there would exist a sequence $(x_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ in $K$ such that $d_F(\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)(x_n),\widehat{q}(\alpha)(x_n))>\epsilon$ for all $n$. By compactness of $K$ we can assume that $x_n$ converges to some $x^*\in K$ as $nightarrow\infty$. Letting $y_n,y\in\mathrm{Y}$ be such that $x_n=q(y_n)$ for all $n$ and $x^*=q(y)$, by Lemma ef{lem:conv-quot} there would exist a sequence $z_n\in \mathrm{Z}$ such that $y_n+z_nightarrow y$ as $nightarrow\infty$. Renaming this sequence, we could assume that $y_nightarrow y$. By \cite[Theorem 46.10]{Mu} we would then have $\alpha_n(y_n)ightarrow \alpha(y)$ as $nightarrow \infty$. Since $\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)(x_n)=q(\alpha_n(y_n))$ and $\widehat{q}(\alpha)(x^*)=q(\alpha(y))$, by continuity of $q$ we would have $\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)(x_n)ightarrow \widehat{q}(\alpha)(x^*)$ as $nightarrow\infty$, contradicting $d_F(\widehat{q}(\alpha_n)(x_n),\widehat{q}(\alpha)(x_n))>\epsilon$ for $n$ large enough. Next we prove the key fact that $\widehat{q}$ is surjective from $\Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ onto $\Theta_i(F)$, for each $i\in [k]$. Note that for $i=k$ this is clear by virtue of $q$ being a fibration (its $k$-th structure homomorphism is then surjective), so we may assume that $i<k$. Let $\eta_{k-1}:\Theta_i(F)ightarrow\Theta_i(F_{k-1})$ be the homomorphism from \eqref{eq:etaj}. Since $q_{k-1}:\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}ightarrow F_{k-1}$ is an 	extsc{lch}-nilspace isomorphism, for every $\gamma\in \Theta_i(F)$ we can treat $\eta_{k-1}(\gamma)$ as a translation on $\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}$. By Corollary ef{cor:ext-of-lcfr-is-lcfr}, $\mathrm{Y}$ is also a Lie-fibered $k$-step nilspace. Now, we want to apply Proposition ef{prop:cont-lift-of-trans} to prove the existence of a (continuous) translation in $\Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ which is a lift of $\eta_{k-1}(\gamma)$ (viewing the latter as a translation in $\Theta_i(\mathrm{Y}_{k-1})$). To be able to apply Proposition ef{prop:cont-lift-of-trans}, we need the associated translation bundle to be a \emph{split} extension of $\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}$. But since $\mathrm{Y}_{k-1}$ is isomorphic to $F_{k-1}$, this translation bundle is thus a degree-$(k-i)$ extension of $F_{k-1}$, so by the above observation related to \eqref{diag:splt-1}, this extension does split. Hence we can indeed apply Proposition ef{prop:cont-lift-of-trans} and thus we obtain a continuous translation $ eta\in \Theta_i(\mathrm{Y})$ that lifts $\eta_{k-1}(\gamma)$ through $\pi_{k-1}$. Now a remaining issue is that a priori $\widehat{q}( eta)$ may not equal $\gamma$. However, the images of these translations through $\eta_{k-1}$ are both equal to $\eta_{k-1}(\gamma)$. Hence $\widehat{q}( eta)-\gamma:Fightarrow \mathcal{D}_k(\mathrm{Z}_k(F))$ is a well-defined continuous morphism. Let $\phi_k$ be the $k$-th structure morphism of $q$, thus $\phi_k$ is a surjective continuous group homomorphism $\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})ightarrow \mathrm{Z}_k(F)$. These last two groups are Lie groups (by assumption for $\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})$, and by Corollary ef{cor:trans&structFree} for $\mathrm{Z}_k(F)$). By Lemma ef{lem:abcasesplit} we have that $\mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})$ is a \emph{split} topological-group extension of $\mathrm{Z}_k(F)$, so there is a continuous homomorphism $s_k:\mathrm{Z}_k(F)ightarrow \mathrm{Z}_k(\mathrm{Y})$ such that $\phi_k\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} s_k$ is the identity map on $\mathrm{Z}_k(F)$. We can now define the map $\alpha:= eta+s_k\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm}(\gamma-\widehat{q}( eta))\circ\hspace{-0.02 cm} q:\mathrm{Y}ightarrow\mathrm{Y}$. This is a continuous translation on $\mathrm{Y}$, and a simple calculation shows that $\widehat{q}(\alpha)=\gamma$, as required. Having proved that $\widehat{q}$ is a continuous surjective homomorphism, we are nearly ready to define the 